Korn shell on Mentec, 2.11BSD patch level 498
========================================================

Source: https://github.com/jdbruner/ksh
Revision: a7a376b650e1015afa044cbf3576ed78cfac5bd3
This is the PDP-11 port of NetBSD 5.0.2's pdksh 5.2.14, not ksh93.
The full upstream configuration is retained: Korn syntax, functions,
arrays, arithmetic, job control, Emacs and vi editing, brace expansion,
and history. Eight separate-I/D overlays fit the PDP-11.

After building, run from your source directory as an ordinary user:

    ./ksh
    ./ksh -c 'print "$KSH_VERSION"; print $((6*7))'

Choose interactive editing with set -o emacs or set -o vi.
For persistent history, set HISTFILE in ksh, for example:

    HISTFILE=$HOME/.ksh_history
    export HISTFILE

The upstream simple history implementation saves on exit. It does not
merge simultaneous sessions as the optional complex-history backend does.

Clean native build and regression test:

    /bin/sh ./mentec-build.sh

This cleans generated shell outputs, rebuilds the local runtime and
compiler helpers, regenerates dependencies, compiles with native tools,
and runs mentec-tests.ksh using the new executable. Logs are build.log,
depend.log, and tests.log. Builds take several minutes on this machine.
The script selects Makefile.mentec if Makefile is absent. If you have
another Makefile selected, move it aside first. Run from the source
directory; no particular user name or absolute path is required.
For an incremental build and test: make; ./ksh mentec-tests.ksh

Local compatibility support
---------------------------

An initially linked shell failed during startup; native compiler runs
also produced inconsistent errors and crashes. Debugging c0 showed it
unexpectedly reaching address zero (the C startup entry) while parsing
line 74 of table.c. Its stack was at an allocation boundary; the later
"Arg count" message was startup running again with a bogus argc, rather
than an actual source argument-count error. Changing argument filenames
changed where the stack boundary was encountered.

native-tools/crt0-stack.s prefaults startup stack space using a simple
memory access. native-tools/csv-stack.s similarly prefaults 4096 bytes
before each C routine body, for both direct and overlay calls. This keeps
call instructions from encountering the observed stack-growth failure.
It preserves the standard register/overlay frame layout. These are local
copies of the host's runtime sources; installed libraries are untouched.

The same runtime is linked into private c0 and c1 compiler passes using
native compiler object files saved from /usr/src/lib/ccom. The stock cc
driver selects these passes with -B./native-tools/ -t01, retaining -O.
The shell links the local startup and register-save objects as well.
See native-tools/README for provenance and bootstrap details.

Two small source compatibility changes are retained: misc.c copies
option letters through an existing integer temporary, and jobs.c uses a
K&R declaration for its internal kill_job function. Neither removes a
shell feature. Their necessity has not been retested after the runtime
fix; they are retained to preserve the validated shell source.

This pdksh parses an entire -c string before executing it. Defining and
calling an alias within that same string requires a later parse, such as
eval. Aliases on separate lines in scripts and interactive input work.

The source tree does not change the login shell or replace /bin/sh.
Run the executable here. The upstream make install is a separate
administrative step that installs /bin/ksh, its manual page, and an
/etc/shells entry.
